The fix: Arc&gt; <p>The Sheaf language is functionally immutable — values are never mutated in place. Operations always create new tensors. This is the perfect use case for reference counting.</p> <p>One-line type change:</p> <pre><code>// Before
Tensor { data: ArrayD&lt;f64&gt;, dtype: Dtype }

// After
Tensor { data: Arc&lt;ArrayD&lt;f64&gt;&gt;, dtype: Dtype }
</code></pre> <p><code>Clone</code> on <code>Arc&lt;T&gt;</code> is O(1) — an atomic increment of a reference counter, regardless of tensor size. An 8MB weight matrix and a 128-element input vector cost the same: ~1 nanosecond.</p>

Device tensor passthrough <p>After the multi-entry cache fix, the remaining buffer cost is the activation tensor <code>x</code> — it changes every call, so it can never be cached. But there's a subtlety: <code>x</code> at the input of transformer-block layer N is exactly the output of layer N-1. The data is already on the GPU. We're pulling it back to the CPU (D2H), then immediately pushing it back (H2D).</p> <p>The fix: retain output buffer views from the last IREE call. On the next call, before allocating a new buffer, check if any input matches a retained output (by fingerprint). If so, reuse the on-device buffer directly — zero-copy passthrough.</p> <pre><code>// After each IREE call: retain output buffer views
iree_hal_buffer_view_retain(bv);
new_output_bvs.push((fingerprint, bv));

// Before each IREE call: check retained outputs first
let output_hit = prev_outputs.iter()
    .find(|(fp, _)| fp.matches(val))
    .map(|(_, bv)| *bv);
</code></pre> <p>Results on Metal GPU (100 tokens, NanoGPT, cached VMFBs):</p> <pre><code>Before (no passthrough):
  buffers:    385.8ms (12.9%)  [hits: 7763, misses: 637]
→ 3.06s wall

After (output passthrough):
  buffers:     72.4ms ( 2.8%)  [hits: 8302, misses: 98]
→ 2.82s wall
</code></pre> <p>Buffer time: 386ms → 72ms (5.4× reduction). Cache misses: 637 → 98 (−85%). The remaining 98 misses are the genuinely new tensors (initial token embedding, first-call weights). Wall time: 3.06s → 2.82s — modest because GPU compute dominates at 85%.</p> <p>Note: D2H still happens (we need the output values on the CPU for the interpreter). The saving is only on the H2D side: we avoid re-uploading data that's already on the GPU.</p>

The fix <p>Register-level CSE cache on <code>StableHLOEmitter</code>:</p> <pre><code>// In StableHLOEmitter struct:
reduce_mean_cache: HashMap&lt;(Register, usize, bool), (Register, StableHLOType)&gt;,

// In emit_reduce_mean():
let cache_key = (*input, axis_usize, keepdims);
if let Some(cached) = self.reduce_mean_cache.get(&amp;cache_key) {
    return cached.clone();
}
// ... compute mean ...
self.reduce_mean_cache.insert(cache_key, (result_reg, sum_ty.clone()));
</code></pre> <p>When <code>emit_var</code> calls <code>emit_reduce_mean</code> on the same register+axis that was already computed by the explicit <code>(mean x)</code>, it gets the cached result. Zero new MLIR ops emitted.</p>

The unbroadcast problem <p>This was the subtlest bug. In the forward pass, <code>+</code> silently broadcasts:</p> <pre><code>pos_embedding[1, 256, 384] + token_embedding[4, 256, 384]
→ result[4, 256, 384]    (pos_embedding broadcast along dim 0)
</code></pre> <p>In the backward pass, the adjoint of <code>result</code> has shape <code>[4, 256, 384]</code>. If we pass it directly as the gradient for <code>pos_embedding</code>, we get a shape mismatch — the gradient is <code>[4, 256, 384]</code> but the parameter is <code>[1, 256, 384]</code>.</p> <p>The fix: <code>sum_to_shape</code>. After computing each gradient contribution, check if the adjoint's shape differs from the operand's shape. If so, sum over the broadcast dimensions:</p> <pre><code>fn maybe_unbroadcast(adj, target_shape) -&gt; CompiledExpr {
    if adj.shape != target_shape {
        sum_to_shape(adj, target_shape)  // reduce broadcast dims
    } else {
        adj
    }
}
</code></pre> <p><code>sum_to_shape</code> is implemented as a codegen builtin that emits <code>stablehlo.reduce</code> ops: first reducing extra leading dimensions, then reducing dimensions where the target has size 1 (with keepdims). This mirrors what JAX's <code>_unbroadcast</code> does internally.</p> <p>Every binary op (<code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>@</code>) wraps its gradient contributions with <code>maybe_unbroadcast</code>. Without this, roughly half the backward ops would produce shape-mismatched MLIR.</p>

The GELU gradient <p>GELU uses the tanh approximation:</p> <pre><code>gelu(x) = 0.5 * x * (1 + tanh(√(2/π) * (x + 0.044715 * x³)))
</code></pre> <p>Its derivative is not trivial. The gradient rule emits 6 intermediate operations:</p> <pre><code>let k = 0.0356774                           // 3 * 0.044715 * √(2/π)
let inner = √(2/π) * (x + 0.044715 * x³)
let t = tanh(inner)
let sech2 = 1 - t²                          // sech²(inner)
let dinner = √(2/π) * (1 + k * x²)         // d(inner)/dx
gelu_grad = adj * 0.5 * ((1 + t) + x * sech2 * dinner)
</code></pre> <p>Getting this wrong produces gradient explosion — the loss goes to 10²⁰ within a single step. We caught this by checking gradient magnitudes: correct gradients for GPT should be in the 1e-4 to 1e-5 range.</p>
